<BaseLayout title="About | Aaron’s Dev Experiments">
	<section class="topic-header">
		<p class="eyebrow">About</p>

		<h1>About This Site</h1>

		<p class="intro">
			A personal technical notebook for the projects, experiments, and
			engineering problems I spend my free time working through.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="about-content">
		<h2>About Me</h2>

		<p>
			I’m Aaron, a computer science graduate with an interest in software
			engineering, graphics programming, systems work, and game
			technology. My background also includes many years working in
			construction, which probably explains some of my tendency to think
			about software in terms of structure, constraints, and how the
			pieces fit together.
		</p>

		<p>
			I built this site as a place to document the technical work I do
			outside of school and work. Some of it leads to useful systems, some
			of it leads to better questions, and some of it ends up being a
			technical dead end worth documenting anyway.
		</p>

		<h2>What I’m Working On</h2>

		<p>
			Most of the material currently comes from Plane Voxel Lab, an
			experimental C++ / OpenGL project focused on terrain representation,
			rendering, spatial systems, and editable world geometry.
		</p>

		<p>
			The site is intentionally broader than that project, though. As I
			build new things, I plan to use the same space to document other
			software engineering experiments and projects as well.
		</p>

		<h2>Why Document It?</h2>

		<p>
			The process of writing about an implementation forces me to explain
			why a design exists, what problem it was intended to solve, and
			where its limitations became apparent. That makes this site partly a
			project journal and partly an extension of the development process
			itself.
		</p>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
